WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.838
At 9:12 the fraud team flags account A-3101.

2
00:00:05.838 --> 00:00:12.399
Which accounts share a device or a phone number with it, directly or through other accounts?

3
00:00:12.567 --> 00:00:16.694
One hop goes account, identifier, account.

4
00:00:16.694 --> 00:00:18.955
A-3102 is one hop away.

5
00:00:18.955 --> 00:00:20.331
A-3103 is two.

6
00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:25.462
In tables, each link is a row: an account, and an identifier it uses.

7
00:00:25.462 --> 00:00:27.717
There's an index on each column.

8
00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:30.703
One hop crosses the table twice.

9
00:00:30.703 --> 00:00:33.945
One index finds A-3101's identifiers.

10
00:00:33.945 --> 00:00:37.712
The other finds who else uses them: A-3102.

11
00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:39.444
That's a self-join.

12
00:00:39.444 --> 00:00:43.183
Each lookup walks down a B+tree: 3 or 4 pages.

13
00:00:43.367 --> 00:00:48.813
Every hop you allow adds two more joins, and the planner has to guess the size of each one.

14
00:00:48.967 --> 00:00:55.962
And every hop fans out, about 6 times here: 6 rows, then 36, then 216.

15
00:00:55.962 --> 00:00:58.260
Six hops: about 47,000.

16
00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:01.511
A graph store keeps the links the other way round.

17
00:01:01.511 --> 00:01:04.311
Next to each account, a list of what it uses.

18
00:01:04.311 --> 00:01:07.423
Next to each identifier, the accounts that use it.

19
00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:11.332
A hop reads a list and jumps straight to the record it names.

20
00:01:11.332 --> 00:01:16.104
No search, so it costs the same with a thousand accounts or a hundred million.

21
00:01:16.267 --> 00:01:24.849
Neo4j stores each relationship with both its nodes, so following one is a pointer read: index-free adjacency.

22
00:01:25.033 --> 00:01:28.667
It needs an index only once, to find where the walk starts.

23
00:01:28.867 --> 00:01:32.102
Both designs still pay for every path they explore.

24
00:01:32.102 --> 00:01:34.512
What differs is the price of each hop.

25
00:01:34.733 --> 00:01:40.349
In tables, it's two tree descents per row, and the trees get deeper as the table grows.

26
00:01:40.349 --> 00:01:43.835
In a graph store, it's two pointer reads, at any size.

27
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:48.174
So keep one or two hops of a known shape in Postgres or Oracle.

28
00:01:48.174 --> 00:01:55.064
For paths of unknown length, rings and cycles, a graph store walks them, and you pay for what you touch.
